=== layout: section
# Discussion Points

=== layout: one-panel
# Setting Your Name in Zoom

@body

- Use your proper first name or a consistent nickname.
- Discussion marks are assigned by sorting names alphabetically by first name.
- Do not use:
  - Start with your last name (e.g., "Smith Jane").
  - Use something obscure (e.g., "iPhone User").
  - Use a different name each time (consistency is key).

- How to Change Your Name\:
  - Click on the "Participants" button at the bottom of the meeting window.
  - Hover over your name in the participant list and click "More."
  - Select "Rename."
  - Enter your new name and click "OK."

=== layout: one-panel
# Change Name Before a Meeting

@body

- Open the Zoom desktop client or the mobile app.
- Go to your profile by clicking on your profile picture or initials in the top-right corner.
- Select "Profile" from the dropdown menu.
- Click "Edit" next to your name.
- Enter your preferred name and then click "Save."

=== layout: one-panel
# [How to Earn Discussion Marks]{color=blue}

@body

- Participate in icebreaker activities at the start of class.
- Engage in the class chat by responding to direct questions during the lecture.
- Provide thoughtful answers to open-ended questions about the material.
- Ask clarifying questions if something is unclear.
- Share personal experiences that relate to the course content.
- Post relevant news articles or resources that connect to the topics we discuss.
- Be on time for the Zoom meeting to earn discussion marks for punctuality.
- Adding emoji tags to another student's comment earns 0.1 points, up to 10 points.

=== layout: one-panel
# [Discussion Marks FAQ]{color=blue}

@body

- 1. Discussion Marks are mostly [based on the chat]{color=red}
  - Not all comments in the chat count, such as "hi", "thanks", or "yes"
  - You can earn up to 6 discussion marks from the chat
- 2. Answering Polls does NOT count
  - It is not easy for me to obtain that data
- 3. Being present in the Zoom meeting when class time starts
  - You can earn up to 2 discussion marks for [being on-time to class]{color=red}
- 4. Direct messages do not count

=== layout: one-panel

# Weekly Talking Marks

@body

- Talking Marks (1-6 talking marks)
  - Before each lecture week, I will distribute a list of topics for students to read about in the textbook and with online and other resources.
  - At the beginning of class, each student will be randomly assigned a topic and will talk about their topic from their seat.
  - I will provide next week's talking points at the end of each class, including today.
- Biotech news (1-4 talking marks)

